    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Part A — Baselines"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Q1. Before any model: what accuracy does a model that always predicts the majority class\n",
    "get? This is the number every result must beat.\n",
    "\n",
    "<details><summary>hint</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "`DummyClassifier(strategy='most_frequent')`. Quoting this number unprompted is a strong signal in an interview.\n",
    "\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "dummy accuracy: 0.6145251396648045\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.dummy import DummyClassifier\n",
    "\n",
    "dummy = DummyClassifier(strategy='most_frequent').fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
    "print('dummy accuracy:', dummy.score(X_test, y_test))\n",
    "\n",
    "# ~0.62 just from always saying \"died\". Any model at 65% is barely doing anything."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Q2. Fit a logistic regression in a pipeline. Report train and test accuracy.\n",
    "Is it beating the dummy?\n",
    "\n",
    "<details><summary>hint</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "Wrap `make_preprocessor()` and the classifier in a `Pipeline`. Use `max_iter=1000` — the default 100 won't converge and will warn at you.\n",
    "\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "train: 0.8300561797752809\n",
      "test : 0.8268156424581006\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n",
    "\n",
    "logreg = Pipeline([('pre', make_preprocessor()),\n",
    "                   ('clf', LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, random_state=42))])\n",
    "logreg.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
    "\n",
    "print('train:', logreg.score(X_train, y_train))\n",
    "print('test :', logreg.score(X_test, y_test))\n",
    "\n",
    "# ~0.83 test vs 0.62 dummy -- a real +21 points. And train/test are close, so it isn't\n",
    "# overfitting. This is now the number a fancier model has to beat."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Part B — Cross-validation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Q3. A single split on 891 rows is noisy. Cross-validate the logistic regression with\n",
    "5-fold stratified CV. Report mean **and** std. Why `StratifiedKFold` and not `KFold`?\n",
    "\n",
    "<details><summary>hint</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "`cross_val_score(pipe, X_train, y_train, cv=cv)`. The std is what tells you whether a difference between two models is real.\n",
    "\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "fold scores: [0.825 0.818 0.852 0.775 0.866]\n",
      "CV accuracy: 0.827 +/- 0.032\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score, StratifiedKFold\n",
    "\n",
    "cv = StratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)\n",
    "scores = cross_val_score(logreg, X_train, y_train, cv=cv, scoring='accuracy')\n",
    "print('fold scores:', scores.round(3))\n",
    "print(f'CV accuracy: {scores.mean():.3f} +/- {scores.std():.3f}')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Stratified keeps the ~38% survival rate in every fold. With plain KFold a fold can end\n",
    "# up with a skewed class mix, which adds variance to the estimate for no reason.\n",
    "# The std (~0.02-0.03) is the real point: two models 1 point apart are indistinguishable."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
